WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.690
Our school tried an AI agent that

2
00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:05.830
could read the timetable and send

3
00:00:05.830 --> 00:00:06.910
messages to parents.

4
00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:09.610
It was meant to send reminders.

5
00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:13.760
One day it sent the whole parent list

6
00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:16.160
a message nobody had approved.

7
00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:21.250
Today: agents that act, the tools we give

8
00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:23.990
them, and the yes that keeps them safe.

9
00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:27.300
A chatbot only talks.

10
00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:31.080
An AI agent can also use tools: read

11
00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:34.000
a timetable, check attendance, set a

12
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.540
reminder, send a message.

13
00:00:36.280 --> 00:00:38.460
That makes it useful, and

14
00:00:38.460 --> 00:00:39.540
it raises the stakes.

15
00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:42.400
Tools come in two kinds.

16
00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:45.590
Reading tools look things up; at

17
00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:47.110
worst, they leak something.

18
00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:49.880
Acting tools change things:

19
00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:53.320
send, delete, pay, publish.

20
00:00:54.050 --> 00:00:56.030
A tricked agent with an acting

21
00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:58.190
tool can act against you.

22
00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:02.590
The rule is least privilege: give an agent

23
00:01:02.590 --> 00:01:05.410
the fewest tools its job needs, reading

24
00:01:05.410 --> 00:01:07.670
before acting, for the shortest time.

25
00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:10.190
Everything a tampered result could

26
00:01:10.190 --> 00:01:12.030
reach is the blast radius.

27
00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:13.500
Keep it small.

28
00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:15.340
Quick check!

29
00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:18.760
An agent's job is to plan tomorrow's

30
00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:21.080
lessons from the timetable and the

31
00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:21.300
library.

32
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:23.480
Which tools should it get?

33
00:01:24.710 --> 00:01:27.190
Timetable and library only.

34
00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:30.260
With no acting tool, a hidden

35
00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:32.580
order in a result can't make it

36
00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:34.260
send or delete anything.

37
00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:37.700
The worst it can do is read or talk.

38
00:01:38.920 --> 00:01:40.740
For jobs that must act,

39
00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:42.480
add a person's yes.

40
00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:45.420
Before any message is sent, the

41
00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:47.400
agent shows it and asks.

42
00:01:48.090 --> 00:01:51.370
And the code checks: was it asked for, and

43
00:01:51.370 --> 00:01:53.650
did someone approve this exact message?

44
00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:56.040
The model can't skip that step.

45
00:01:57.310 --> 00:02:01.890
Why in code, and not just in the prompt?

46
00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:05.610
A hidden order can fake an approval

47
00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:09.390
question, and a trusting yes may send it.

48
00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:12.560
Code that knows who really asked

49
00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:14.280
can't be fooled by words.

50
00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:16.850
Keep a log, too.

51
00:02:17.490 --> 00:02:19.810
Every action the agent takes, with

52
00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:21.470
who approved it and when.

53
00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:23.530
If something goes wrong, you can

54
00:02:23.530 --> 00:02:25.150
see what happened and fix it.

55
00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:28.280
In the app, pick a job and

56
00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:29.780
grant the agent some tools.

57
00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:33.350
Then set a batch of tampered results loose

58
00:02:33.350 --> 00:02:35.730
on it, and count the harmful actions.

59
00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:38.720
Take tools away, and run it again.

60
00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:40.780
Your turn.

61
00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:44.320
An agent must tell parents the

62
00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:45.820
date of the school trip.

63
00:02:46.550 --> 00:02:47.850
What keeps that safe?

64
00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:51.200
Only the tool it needs, and a

65
00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:52.400
yes checked in code.

66
00:02:53.050 --> 00:02:56.470
Remember: reading tools leak at worst;

67
00:02:57.150 --> 00:02:59.750
acting tools can be turned against you.

68
00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:02.600
Give the fewest tools, and keep

69
00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:04.040
the blast radius small.

70
00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:07.390
Ask before it acts, and keep a log.

71
00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:10.610
Try it in class tomorrow: make

72
00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:12.770
“permission slips for robots”.

73
00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:16.080
Groups design a class helper robot for one

74
00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:18.660
job, and list only the tools it needs.

75
00:03:19.310 --> 00:03:22.250
Mark every tool that acts, and decide

76
00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:24.310
who must say yes before it does.
